# Conclusion: Some methodological notes

This report can only provide snapshots of the extensive questionnaire data collected by Cités Unies. We aimed to cover a broad range of topics and variables in order to reveal potentially informative patterns and trends. Rather than summarizing these patterns once more, we conclude with several methodological notes that highlight opportunities to improve future impact evaluations based on the questionnaires.

First, the questionnaires could likely be shorter. Reducing the number of questions would probably decrease attrition: volunteers who respond to a very long questionnaire at q1 may be less motivated to complete another long questionnaire at q2, let alone q3. Shorter questionnaires could also improve response quality. When questionnaires are lengthy, respondents may become tired or bored and begin answering quickly and without much attention. Another option to improve response quality would be to include attention checks. That said, because volunteers are not required to take the survey, it is reasonable to assume that those who do participate are generally motivated to complete it in good faith.

Another potential improvement would be to make the questionnaires at different time points more similar. Asking the same questions across multiple waves makes it possible to track how individual volunteers change over the course of their service civique (as done in @sec-within-change).

On a minor note, it may be more informative to ask volunteers about their voting intentions rather than their past voting behavior. There are indications that some volunteers may not interpret the current question about past voting behavior as intended (@fig-vote_derniers_elections-youngest). In addition, changes in past voting behavior are inherently dependent on whether elections happen to occur for a particular cohort during their year of service civique.

Finally, I would like to reiterate a point already raised in the introduction: the questionnaire data are purely observational, and none of the associations presented in this report support specific causal interpretations. For example, in the section on demographic differences between programs (@sec-programs), programs with older volunteers also tend to have more highly educated volunteers. These observations are likely related, since older participants have simply had more time to pursue education. As another example, @fig-satisfaction-programs suggests that fewer volunteers report being "very satisfied" in key programs compared to other programs (listed in the figure under "NA"). However, key programs have attracted an increasing number of volunteers over time (@fig-programs-overview), and some of them are relatively recent. At the same time, the share of volunteers who report being "very satisfied" has declined slightly across cohorts (@fig-satisfaction). It is therefore unclear whether the observed differences are driven by the programs themselves, broader cohort trends in satisfaction, or other unobserved factors.


